01:01:44.510 --> 01:01:58.380
<v Ira Glass>Thanks, as always, to our program's cofounder, Mr. Torey Malatia. He does not understand why people at the office did not get his Halloween costume last year. He's like, isn't it obvious? A rhinoceros on St. Patrick's Day.
<v Ira Glass>一如既往，感谢我们项目的联合创始人托雷·马拉蒂亚先生。他不明白为什么办公室的人去年没看懂他的万圣节服装。他说，这不是很明显吗？圣帕特里克节的犀牛。

01:01:58.380 --> 01:01:59.870
<v Majid Khan>I was a greenhorn.
<v Majid Khan>我当时是个雏儿。

01:01:59.870 --> 01:02:03.870
<v Ira Glass>I'm Ira Glass. Back next week with more stories of This American Life.
<v Ira Glass>我是艾拉·格拉斯。下周带着更多《美国生活》的故事回来。

01:02:03.870 --> 01:02:24.440
<v Ira Glass>[BECK, "MODERN GUILT"]
<v Ira Glass>[BECK, "MODERN GUILT"]

01:02:24.440 --> 01:02:41.730
<v Ira Glass>Next week on the podcast of This American Life. Most of the people imprisoned at Guantánamo were low-level fighters at best. More than 95% were never charged with a crime. But if there was any case at Guantánamo where you think justice would be done, it was the trial for the man accused of orchestrating 9/11.
<v Ira Glass>下周在《美国生活》的播客中。关塔那摩关押的大多数人充其量是低级别的战士。超过95%的人从未被指控犯罪。但如果关塔那摩有任何案件让你觉得会伸张正义，那就是对那名被指控策划9/11事件的人的审判。

01:02:41.730 --> 01:02:46.730
<v Ira Glass>So, more than two decades later, how's it going with that trial? Answer-- even that's messed up in some really shocking ways. Next week on the podcast on your local public radio station.
<v Ira Glass>那么，二十多年过去了，那场审判进行得如何？答案——即使是那样也以一些非常令人震惊的方式搞砸了。下周在您当地公共广播电台的播客上。
